<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jenny Shipley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. <a href="/years/1952">1952</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jenny Shipley is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> politician who became the country's first woman Prime Minister in December 1997, after replacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jim-bolger">Jim Bolger</a> as leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>. Earlier, as social welfare minister in 1990–1991, she cut benefits as part of the National government's spending reductions. Her coalition with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand-first">New Zealand First</a> collapsed in 1998, and she lost the 1999 election to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helen-clark">Helen Clark</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jenny-shipley.jpg" alt="Jenny Shipley" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: New Zealand Government, Office of the Governor-General · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jenny_Shipley_2020_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
